 "lines": [
  {
   "i": 0,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "In a pitch-black lab, the story goes, a professor saw the bones inside his own living hand.",
   "from": 0,
   "to": 124
  },
  {
   "i": 1,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "November eighth, eighteen ninety-five. Wilhelm R\u00f6ntgen was testing a cathode-ray tube.",
   "from": 128,
   "to": 253
  },
  {
   "i": 2,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "He wrapped it in black cardboard, so no light could escape.",
   "from": 257,
   "to": 341
  },
  {
   "i": 3,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "But across the room, a screen of barium platinocyanide glowed green.",
   "from": 345,
   "to": 437
  },
  {
   "i": 4,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "He blocked it with a thousand-page book, then a board. Still it glowed.",
   "from": 441,
   "to": 549
  },
  {
   "i": 5,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "Then his hand passed in front, and the screen showed his bones.",
   "from": 553,
   "to": 665
  },
  {
   "i": 6,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "He spent seven weeks testing the rays.",
   "from": 669,
   "to": 717
  },
  {
   "i": 7,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "On December twenty-second, his wife Anna Bertha laid her hand on a photographic plate.",
   "from": 721,
   "to": 826
  },
  {
   "i": 8,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "Her bones and wedding ring showed. She reportedly cried out.",
   "from": 829,
   "to": 930
  },
  {
   "i": 9,
   "speaker": "anna",
   "text": "I have seen my death!",
   "from": 934,
   "to": 981
  },
  {
   "i": 10,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "So how does a ray pass through skin, but not bone?",
   "from": 985,
   "to": 1064
  },
  {
   "i": 11,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "You see, fast electrons inside the tube slam into the glass and make invisible rays.",
   "from": 1080,
   "to": 1198
  },
  {
   "i": 12,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "Skin lets those rays through. Bone and metal block them and cast a shadow.",
   "from": 1202,
   "to": 1297
  },
  {
   "i": 13,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "He named them X-rays, X for unknown, and sent off his paper on December twenty-eighth.",
   "from": 1300,
   "to": 1410
  },
  {
   "i": 14,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "Within weeks the news circled the world, and doctors were photographing broken bones.",
   "from": 1414,
   "to": 1531
  },
  {
   "i": 15,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "In nineteen oh one, he won the first Nobel Prize in physics. He never patented the rays.",
   "from": 1535,
   "to": 1662
  },
  {
   "i": 16,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "Every X-ray you've ever had began with one green glow in the dark.",
   "from": 1665,
   "to": 1752
  }
 ],
